"# Appendix H — The composition summit (verbs folded into one game, no modules)

The other dialect's capstone composes *seven modules* — `graphics/2d`, `input/keys`, `sound/samples`, `physics/2d`, `entities/basic`, `random/uniform`, `game/loop` — into one small game via seven `import` lines. **Motoi has no `import` and no modules.** The same game, in Motoi, is one file of top-level `define`s that fold small helper verbs together (Chapter 7). This appendix shows that shape with runnable code: the composition is real, only the module wrapper is gone.

The game is *avoid the falling blocks*: blocks fall, the player dodges, a collision ends it. Here we build the state-and-logic core as plain verbs — no framebuffer, so it stays runnable and gradeable — and each piece has one job, exactly as a module would.

## The pieces, as verbs

Each \"module\" the other dialect would import becomes a few small verbs. An entity is a tagged list; physics is a step verb; collision is its own verb:

```scheme
(define (make-block x y vy) (list 'block x y vy))     ; entities
(define (block-x b)  (list-ref b 1))
(define (block-y b)  (list-ref b 2))
(define (block-vy b) (list-ref b 3))

(define (step-block b)                                 ; physics: apply velocity
  (make-block (block-x b) (+ (block-y b) (block-vy b)) (block-vy b)))

(define (on-screen? b) (< (block-y b) 600))            ; cull off-screen

(define (collides? player b)                           ; collision test
  (and (< (abs (- (list-ref player 1) (block-x b))) 20)
       (< (abs (- (list-ref player 2) (block-y b))) 20)))

(list (step-block (make-block 100 0 3))
      (on-screen? (make-block 100 700 3)))
;; ⇒ ((block 100 3 3) #f)
```

Each verb has one job — make a block, step it, cull it, test collision — the same single-purpose split the other dialect got from separate modules, achieved here with separate `define`s calling the always-loaded standard library (`list-ref`, `abs`, `<`).

## The frame update, folded

The frame logic is one verb that folds the pieces: step every block, drop the off-screen ones, and check for a hit. `map` and `filter` are already loaded:

```scheme
(define player (list 'player 400 550 0))
(define blocks (list (make-block 400 540 3) (make-block 100 0 3)))

(define (update bs)                                    ; the frame fold
  (filter on-screen? (map step-block bs)))

(define (any-hit? p bs)                                ; mutual-recursion-free scan
  (cond ((null? bs) #f)
        ((collides? p (car bs)) #t)
        (else (any-hit? p (cdr bs)))))

(list (update blocks) (any-hit? player blocks))
;; ⇒ (((block 400 543 3) (block 100 3 3)) #t)
```

`update` is short. Almost everything it does is delegated to a helper — `step-block`, `on-screen?`, `collides?`. Its own job is thin: call the pieces in order and fold the results. That is the fold (Chapter 7), and it is exactly what the other dialect's game cart did across modules, done here across verbs.

## What each verb contributes

- **make-block / block-x / block-y / block-vy** — the entity model (the other dialect's `entities/basic`).
- **step-block** — velocity application (its `physics/2d`).
- **on-screen?** — culling (part of the game loop).
- **collides?** — collision (its `physics/2d` again).
- **update / any-hit?** — the frame fold (its `game/loop`).

The graphics, input, and sound \"modules\" would, in a real Motoi game, be more verbs calling framebuffer and input library verbs — no import, just more `define`s. The shape does not change: one job per verb, folded by a thin top-level verb.

## Why this shape works — same reasons, no modules

- The frame fold does not know how a block steps; `step-block` owns that. Change the physics in one verb and every caller gets it.
- The fold does not know how collision is computed; `collides?` owns that.
- Each verb is small enough to understand and test alone.

This is the isolation the other dialect got from module boundaries, achieved in Motoi by lexical scope and single-purpose verbs (Chapters 7 and 9).

## There is no module list from Cortex

The other dialect ended by querying `(cortex/query :predicate 'module-loaded)` for the seven modules and their hashes. **Motoi loads no modules**, so that query returns nothing. If you want to note the pieces your game is made of, you record the fact yourself:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'game-pieces '(block physics collision update))
(cortex/recall 'game-pieces)
;; ⇒ a memory record whose :data is (block physics collision update)
```

## Try this

Run the two code blocks above and confirm the game core steps blocks, culls the off-screen one, and detects the collision. Then sketch your own small game: list the *verbs* you need (not modules — there are none), give each one job, and write a thin `update` that folds them. If your fold verb is long, a piece is missing; cut it into its own verb. That is the composition summit in Motoi: many small verbs, one thin fold, no import in sight.

The base is small. The standard library is always present. The program is the fold.")
